{"reason": "The initial plan's pseudo-labeling strategy is sound but requires modifications to avoid overfitting and computational overhead. Historical successful experiments show that unfreezing the backbone with differential learning rates and a warm-up phase, combined with a larger training set (using all training data), significantly improved performance (val log-loss 0.3803). The failed experiment removed features but still used a frozen backbone for too long, which plateaued. The current execution shows validation log-loss plateauing at 0.4356 with a frozen backbone and only 80% of training data. The refined plan will: (1) simplify pseudo-labeling by using a single model trained on all training data (instead of 5-fold cross-validation) to generate pseudo-labels, reducing computational cost; (2) unfreeze the backbone after a warm-up phase with differential learning rates to improve representation learning; (3) remove engineered features to simplify the model and reduce memory, as historical success showed; (4) reduce max_length to 128 to save memory and allow larger effective batch size; (5) increase early stopping patience to 6. These modifications address the representation bottleneck and leverage historical successes.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove all engineered features (char/word counts, n-grams, TF-IDF, etc.) and only use raw text for the transformer model. WHY: Historical successful experiments without engineered features achieved better performance (0.3803 val log-loss) compared to current plateau (0.4356). Engineered features add noise and require significant preprocessing, and the transformer already learns rich representations from text. HOW: In the data processing section, skip all feature engineering steps. Directly tokenize raw text from train_df and test_df. Keep the label encoder and StratifiedKFold split (but use full training data for final model). Interface constraints: preserve variable names like train_texts, val_texts, test_texts, train_labels_encoded, etc. The feature extraction functions and scaler can be removed entirely.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: (1) Implement pseudo-labeling: train a model on all training data (full 17621 samples) with a mini validation set (500 samples) for early stopping, then use this model to predict test set probabilities, select top 500 high-confidence test samples as pseudo-labels, and retrain on augmented data (full train + pseudo-labeled test). (2) Unfreeze the DeBERTa backbone after a warm-up of 5 epochs with frozen backbone, then use differential learning rates (1e-6 for backbone, 2e-5 for head) with cosine scheduler and warm-up. (3) Reduce max_length from 256 to 128 to save GPU memory and speed up training. (4) Increase early stopping patience from 4 to 6 to allow more time for fine-tuning. WHY: The initial plan attributes the plateau (0.4356) to frozen backbone and limited training data (80% split). Pseudo-labeling increases effective training size, unfreezing with warm-up and differential LR allows gentle fine-tuning, reducing max_length lowers memory, and increasing patience allows more epochs for convergence. Historical success with these settings achieved 0.3803 val log-loss. HOW: In the training loop, after epoch 5 set requires_grad=True for backbone parameters. Modify optimizer_grouped_parameters to include two groups: backbone (lr=1e-6, weight_decay=0.01) and head (lr=2e-5, weight_decay=0.01). Add a cosine scheduler with warm-up (warmup_steps=5*train_loader_steps, total_steps=num_epochs*train_loader_steps). Set MAX_SEQ_LENGTH=128. Change patience=6. Keep batch_size=8, AdamW, gradient clipping=1.0, mixed precision (AMP). Add pseudo-labeling phase before final training: train initial model on full training data with mini validation, predict test probabilities, select top 500 with max probability>0.9, add them to training set with pseudo-labels, then train final model on augmented set with same early stopping."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's pseudo-labeling strategy is sound but requires modifications to avoid overfitting and computational overhead. Historical successful experiments show that unfreezing the backbone with differential learning rates and a warm-up phase, combined with a larger training set (using all training data), significantly improved performance (val log-loss 0.3803). The failed experiment removed features but still used a frozen backbone for too long, which plateaued. The current execution shows validation log-loss plateauing at 0.4356 with a frozen backbone and only 80% of training data. The refined plan will: (1) simplify pseudo-labeling by using a single model trained on all training data (instead of 5-fold cross-validation) to generate pseudo-labels, reducing computational cost; (2) unfreeze the backbone after a warm-up phase with differential learning rates to improve representation learning; (3) remove engineered features to simplify the model and reduce memory, as historical success showed; (4) reduce max_length to 128 to save memory and allow larger effective batch size; (5) increase early stopping patience to 6. These modifications address the representation bottleneck and leverage historical successes.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove all engineered features (char/word counts, n-grams, TF-IDF, etc.) and only use raw text for the transformer model. WHY: Historical successful experiments without engineered features achieved better performance (0.3803 val log-loss) compared to current plateau (0.4356). Engineered features add noise and require significant preprocessing, and the transformer already learns rich representations from text. HOW: In the data processing section, skip all feature engineering steps. Directly tokenize raw text from train_df and test_df. Keep the label encoder and StratifiedKFold split (but use full training data for final model). Interface constraints: preserve variable names like train_texts, val_texts, test_texts, train_labels_encoded, etc. The feature extraction functions and scaler can be removed entirely.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: (1) Implement pseudo-labeling: train a model on all training data (full 17621 samples) with a mini validation set (500 samples) for early stopping, then use this model to predict test set probabilities, select top 500 high-confidence test samples as pseudo-labels, and retrain on augmented data (full train + pseudo-labeled test). (2) Unfreeze the DeBERTa backbone after a warm-up of 5 epochs with frozen backbone, then use differential learning rates (1e-6 for backbone, 2e-5 for head) with cosine scheduler and warm-up. (3) Reduce max_length from 256 to 128 to save GPU memory and speed up training. (4) Increase early stopping patience from 4 to 6 to allow more time for fine-tuning. WHY: The initial plan attributes the plateau (0.4356) to frozen backbone and limited training data (80% split). Pseudo-labeling increases effective training size, unfreezing with warm-up and differential LR allows gentle fine-tuning, reducing max_length lowers memory, and increasing patience allows more epochs for convergence. Historical success with these settings achieved 0.3803 val log-loss. HOW: In the training loop, after epoch 5 set requires_grad=True for backbone parameters. Modify optimizer_grouped_parameters to include two groups: backbone (lr=1e-6, weight_decay=0.01) and head (lr=2e-5, weight_decay=0.01). Add a cosine scheduler with warm-up (warmup_steps=5*train_loader_steps, total_steps=num_epochs*train_loader_steps). Set MAX_SEQ_LENGTH=128. Change patience=6. Keep batch_size=8, AdamW, gradient clipping=1.0, mixed precision (AMP). Add pseudo-labeling phase before final training: train initial model on full training data with mini validation, predict test probabilities, select top 500 with max probability>0.9, add them to training set with pseudo-labels, then train final model on augmented set with same early stopping."
